# =============================================================================
# CiviCRM Standalone dev box  --  JPS / Cloud Scripting manifest
# =============================================================================
# The "install image". Install it via the Dashboard:
#   Dashboard > Import > Import manifest, then paste this URL:
#   https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RaggedStaff/civi-dev-box/main/jps/civi-standalone.jps
#
# There is no `jps` CLI - JPS is the manifest format, not a command. For a
# scripted install see "Deploying" in the README, which uses the Jelastic REST
# API endpoint environment/control/importmanifest.
#
# Target: app.jpe.infomaniak.com (Jelastic PaaS, public provider)
# Site:   https://dev.civi.sioldata.com
#
# Stack (deliberately pinned):
#   app server : apache (Apache + PHP)
#   php        : 8.5.11
#   database   : mariadb-dockerized 11.8.9 (LTS)
#   civicrm    : 6.18.2 Standalone
#
# WHY PHP 8.5
#   Two independent reasons, which is why this was not a close call:
#
#   1. CiviCRM pairs the versions explicitly. Its requirements page says
#      "PHP: PHP 8.3, 8.4 or 8.5 is recommended (8.4 runs with CiviCRM
#      6.12-6.15, 8.5 with 6.16+)". This box runs 6.18.2, so 8.5 is the
#      matched pair.
#
#   2. dfc_civicrm permits it:
#      <php_compatibility>8.1 8.2 8.3 8.4 8.5</php_compatibility>
#
#   HISTORY, because it was the other way round until 2026-10-08: this manifest
#   pinned 8.4 and cited the extension as the reason, because the reference
#   info.xml on docs.civicrm.org listed only 8.1-8.4, which made 8.4 act as a
#   ceiling. The extension lifted it - its own tools/preflight.sh records this -
#   so the rationale above replaced the old one rather than contradicting it.
#
#   CiviCRM enforces the declared php_compatibility range at registration, so
#   raising phpTag past 8.5 without first adding <ver>8.6</ver> to the
#   extension's info.xml will break `cv ext:enable`.
#
# WHAT THIS MANIFEST DOES *NOT* DO
#   It does not deploy the extension. The extension has no git remote and its
#   vendor/ + composer.lock are gitignored, so there is nothing to fetch here.
#   It is deployed afterwards as a built archive - see README and
#   `make ext-install` / `make ext-verify`. The onInstall step below still
#   supports a source URL for completeness, but expect to leave it blank.
# =============================================================================
type: install
version: 1.0
name: CiviCRM Standalone Dev Box (dfc_civicrm)
description: |
  CiviCRM Standalone on Apache/PHP + MariaDB, tuned to CiviCRM's
  documented requirements, for testing the dfc_civicrm native extension.

  * CiviCRM 6.18.2 Standalone served from the domain root (as required)
  * PHP 8.5.11, MariaDB 11.8.9
  * Database tuned: sql_mode without ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY/ANSI, utf8mb4,
    thread_stack 256k, trusted function creators
  * private/, public/, ext/ on persistent volumes; survive redeploy
  * Release code re-materialised automatically on container restart
  * Demo data is a deployment-time choice

  The extension itself is deployed separately as a built archive - the
  manifest deliberately does not try to fetch it.
homepage: https://dev.civi.sioldata.com
appVersion: 6.18.2
displayName: ${settings.envName}
skipNodeEmails: true

categories:
  - cms
  - php
  - crm

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployment-time choices
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
settings:
  fields:
    - caption: Environment name
      type: string
      name: envName
      default: civi-dev
      hideLabel: false
      description: Name shown in the dashboard for this environment.

    - caption: Site URL
      type: string
      name: siteUrl
      default: "https://dev.civi.sioldata.com"
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        Baked into the install by `cv core:install --cms-base-url`. Must be the
        final stable URL - changing it later means re-installing.

    - caption: Install CiviCRM demo (sample) data
      type: checkbox
      name: demoData
      default: false
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        Off = empty site, installs in ~1 minute. On = realistic
        contacts/contributions to test against, much slower.

    - caption: CiviCRM version
      type: string
      name: civicrmVersion
      default: 6.18.2
      hideLabel: false

    - caption: PHP tag
      type: string
      name: phpTag
      default: "8.5.11"
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        8.5 is what CiviCRM pairs with releases 6.16+, and dfc_civicrm declares
        php_compatibility 8.1-8.5. Confirm this exact patch tag exists on your
        panel before installing - the Cloud Scripting reference lists only PHP
        7.4/8.0/8.1 as engines and is stale. Raising this above 8.5 needs
        <ver>8.6</ver> added to the extension's info.xml first, because CiviCRM
        enforces the declared range at registration.

    - caption: MariaDB tag
      type: string
      name: mariadbTag
      default: "11.8.9"
      hideLabel: false
      description: 11.4 or 11.8 LTS recommended by CiviCRM.

    - caption: PHP cloudlets
      type: spinner
      name: cpCloudlets
      increment: 2
      default: 8
      min: 4
      description: CiviCRM is memory hungry. 8 recommended, 4 is the minimum.

    - caption: MariaDB cloudlets
      type: spinner
      name: dbCloudlets
      increment: 2
      default: 4
      min: 4

    - caption: Administrator email
      type: string
      name: adminEmail
      default: admin@sioldata.com
      hideLabel: false

    - caption: Administrator password
      type: password
      name: adminPass
      default: civiAdmin!2026
      hideLabel: false

    - caption: Database password
      type: password
      name: dbPass
      default: civiDb!2026
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        Supplied rather than generated so the database node and the PHP node use
        the same credentials without cross-node plumbing.

    - caption: MariaDB root password (usually leave blank)
      type: password
      name: dbRootPass
      default: ""
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        The platform generates its own MariaDB root password. Leave this BLANK
        and 10-db-tune.sh will discover it on its own - from /root/.my.cnf, the
        platform's my.cnf locations, or passwordless socket auth - which works on
        most nodes.

        Only fill this in if the install stops with "cannot authenticate to
        MariaDB as an administrator". The value is on the database node under
        Credentials in the dashboard.

    - caption: Persistent data directory
      type: string
      name: dataDir
      default: /var/lib/civicrm-data
      hideLabel: false

    - caption: Extension key
      type: string
      name: extKey
      default: "dfc_civicrm"
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        Must equal the <key> in the extension's info.xml AND the directory name
        CiviCRM resolves to (<ext-dir>/<key>/<file>.php).

    - caption: Extension source (optional - usually leave blank)
      type: string
      name: extSource
      default: ""
      hideLabel: false
      description: |
        Only for a remote-pushed copy of the extension. dfc_civicrm has no git
        remote and its vendor/ is gitignored, so prefer the archive workflow:
        `make ext-install`. Blank = deploy the site only.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Topology
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
nodes:
  # Apache + PHP, not NGINX.
  #
  # CiviCRM's Standalone release ships .htaccess files - that is how it keeps
  # private/ unreadable (private/ holds civicrm.settings.php, i.e. the DB
  # credentials) and routes /civicrm/* through its front controller. Apache
  # honours those rules as shipped; NGINX ignores .htaccess and would need them
  # reimplemented by hand in a vhost.
  #
  # dfc_civicrm registers its own routes via xml/Menu/dfc_civicrm.xml, so it
  # needs no rewriting of its own - but CiviCRM core still does.
  #
  # nodeType `apache` is the documented Cloud Scripting identifier for the
  # Apache+PHP template (alias `apache2`). There is no "apachephp-dockerized".
  - nodeType: apache
    nodeGroup: cp
    displayName: CiviCRM
    engine: php8.5
    tag: ${settings.phpTag}
    cloudlets: ${settings.cpCloudlets}
    scalingMode: STATELESS
    diskLimit: 20
    isRedeploySupport: true
    isDeploySupport: false
    volumes:
      - ${settings.dataDir}
    links:
      - sqldb:DB
    env:
      CIVICRM_DATA_DIR: ${settings.dataDir}
      CIVICRM_VERSION: ${settings.civicrmVersion}
      CIVICRM_SITE_URL: ${settings.siteUrl}
      CIVICRM_DB_NAME: civicrm
      CIVICRM_DB_USER: civicrm
      CIVICRM_DB_PASS: ${settings.dbPass}
      CIVICRM_DB_PORT: "3306"
      CIVICRM_ADMIN_USER: admin
      CIVICRM_ADMIN_PASS: ${settings.adminPass}
      CIVICRM_ADMIN_EMAIL: ${settings.adminEmail}
      CIVICRM_DEMO_DATA: ${settings.demoData}
      CIVICRM_EXT_KEY: ${settings.extKey}
      CIVICRM_EXT_SOURCE: ${settings.extSource}

  - nodeType: mariadb-dockerized
    nodeGroup: sqldb
    displayName: MariaDB
    tag: ${settings.mariadbTag}
    cloudlets: ${settings.dbCloudlets}
    diskLimit: 20
    scalingMode: STATEFUL
    isRedeploySupport: true
    isDeploySupport: false
    volumes:
      - /var/lib/mysql
      - /var/log/mysql
    # 10-db-tune.sh creates the schema and user from these. Without them it
    # would die with "CIVICRM_DB_PASS must be provided" - it reads the
    # environment, never positional arguments.
    #
    # CIVICRM_DB_ROOT_PASS is OPTIONAL and empty by default. The platform
    # generates its own MariaDB root password, which 10-db-tune.sh tries to
    # discover (via /root/.my.cnf, the platform's cnf locations, then passwordless
    # socket auth). Set this field to that root password if discovery fails:
    # Dashboard > the DB node > Credentials.
    env:
      CIVICRM_DB_NAME: civicrm
      CIVICRM_DB_USER: civicrm
      CIVICRM_DB_PASS: ${settings.dbPass}
      CIVICRM_MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD: ${settings.dbRootPass}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Provisioning
#
# Scripts are fetched over HTTP from ${baseUrl}. Point baseUrl at a public raw
# URL for this repo, or override it as an extra setting on the API import call
# (see "Deploying" in the README).
#
# Without a reachable baseUrl the environment still creates; only the
# provisioning steps are skipped. You can then run the same scripts by hand over
# SSH:  make up
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
baseUrl: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/raggedstaff/civi-dev-box/main/

onInstall:

  # --- 0. Fetch the scripts to a real directory ----------------------------
  # Scripts MUST be run as files, not piped into `bash -s`.
  #
  # When bash reads a script from stdin, BASH_SOURCE is unset - $0 is just
  # "bash". Every script's `. lib.sh` line then resolved to "/lib.sh", so the
  # whole install died with:
  #     bash: line 9: BASH_SOURCE[0]: unbound variable
  #     bash: line 9: /lib.sh: No such file or directory
  # Writing to $d fixes that and puts lib.sh next to each script, which is
  # where the scripts look for it.
  #
  # curl || wget because the mariadb-dockerized image may not ship curl. The
  # trailing `|| exit 1` matters: a silent fetch failure would otherwise leave a
  # zero-byte script and fail later with a much more confusing message.
  - cmd [cp]: d="$HOME/.civi-scripts"; mkdir -p "$d"; for f in lib.sh 05-enable-php-ext.sh 06-disable-default-auth.sh 00-preflight.sh 20-fetch-civicrm.sh 30-install-civicrm.sh 40-install-extension.sh 90-healthcheck.sh; do curl -fsS "${baseUrl}scripts/$f" -o "$d/$f" || wget -qO "$d/$f" "${baseUrl}scripts/$f" || exit 1; done

  - cmd [sqldb]: d="$HOME/.civi-scripts"; mkdir -p "$d"; for f in lib.sh 10-db-tune.sh; do curl -fsS "${baseUrl}scripts/$f" -o "$d/$f" || wget -qO "$d/$f" "${baseUrl}scripts/$f" || exit 1; done

  # --- 1. Enable the PHP extensions the platform ships but does not load ----
  # The image has intl disabled and CiviCRM requires it ("PHP INTL - required
  # for outputting localized formatted number strings from CiviCRM 5.28
  # onwards"), so preflight would refuse. The platform's PHP Extensions docs
  # list intl.so among the dynamic extensions available to Apache PHP, so the
  # usual outcome is that the binary is already on disk and just needs
  # enabling. This discovers the paths at runtime rather than assuming
  # /usr/lib64/php/modules or /usr/local/etc/php.
  - cmd [cp]: bash "$HOME/.civi-scripts/05-enable-php-ext.sh"

  # --- 2. Fail fast if the runtime does not satisfy CiviCRM --------------
  # Note the deliberate absence of `|| die '...'`. die() is a function from our
  # own lib.sh; it does not exist in the shell Jelastic runs cmd entries in, so
  # using it replaced the real exit status with "die: command not found" (127)
  # and hid the actual failure. A non-zero exit already aborts the install.
  - cmd [cp]: bash "$HOME/.civi-scripts/00-preflight.sh"

  # --- 3. Remove the provider's default HTTP Basic Auth -----------------
  # The Apache PHP template ships with basic auth enabled so a fresh container
  # is not left serving to the world. Until it is removed, every request to the
  # site returns 401 and the browser prompts for credentials.
  - cmd [cp]: bash "$HOME/.civi-scripts/06-disable-default-auth.sh"

  # --- 4. Database: tuning + schema/user --------------------------------
  # No arguments: 10-db-tune.sh reads CIVICRM_DB_NAME / _USER / _PASS from the
  # environment (see the sqldb node's env block). It was previously handed the
  # password as '$1', which the script never read - so it would have died on an
  # empty password even with the sourcing fixed.
  - cmd [sqldb]: bash "$HOME/.civi-scripts/10-db-tune.sh"

  # --- 5. Release code into the document root + bind volumes ------------
  # CIVICRM_VERSION and CIVICRM_SITE_URL come from the cp node's env block.
  - cmd [cp]: bash "$HOME/.civi-scripts/20-fetch-civicrm.sh"

  # --- 6. Non-interactive install ----------------------------------------
  - cmd [cp]: bash "$HOME/.civi-scripts/30-install-civicrm.sh"

  # --- 7. Optional source-based extension deploy ------------------------
  # Skipped unless extSource is set. The dfc_civicrm archive workflow
  # (make ext-install) is the normal path.
  - if:
      condition: ${settings.extSource}
      then:
        # No `--` separator: with the script as a file, everything after the
        # filename is already a positional parameter, so `bash f -- --link`
        # would pass "$1=--" and the argument parser would reject it.
        - cmd [cp]: bash "$HOME/.civi-scripts/40-install-extension.sh" --link

# Self-heal: the app tree is ephemeral, so re-materialise the release code
# every time the PHP container restarts. Data volumes are untouched, so this is
# cheap and idempotent.
onAfterRestartNode [cp]:
  - cmd [cp]: d="$HOME/.civi-scripts"; mkdir -p "$d"; for f in lib.sh 05-enable-php-ext.sh 06-disable-default-auth.sh 20-fetch-civicrm.sh; do curl -fsS "${baseUrl}scripts/$f" -o "$d/$f" || wget -qO "$d/$f" "${baseUrl}scripts/$f" || exit 1; done
  # Re-enable intl before anything else: if it had to be compiled, it lives in
  # the container filesystem and is gone after a node replacement.
  - cmd [cp]: bash "$HOME/.civi-scripts/05-enable-php-ext.sh"
  # Same for the Apache config - it lives in the container filesystem too, so a
  # node replacement restores the 401 prompt.
  - cmd [cp]: bash "$HOME/.civi-scripts/06-disable-default-auth.sh"
  - cmd [cp]: bash "$HOME/.civi-scripts/20-fetch-civicrm.sh"

startPage: ${settings.siteUrl}/civicrm/login

success: |
  ### CiviCRM dev box is ready

  - **Site**: ${settings.siteUrl}/civicrm/login
  - **Admin**: `${settings.adminEmail}` / your chosen password
  - **PHP**: `${settings.phpTag}` &nbsp;&nbsp; **DB**: `${settings.mariadbTag}` &nbsp;&nbsp; **CiviCRM**: `${settings.civicrmVersion}`
  - **Demo data**: `${settings.demoData}`

  #### 1. Confirm private/ is not web-readable

      curl -sI ${settings.siteUrl}/private/civicrm.settings.php   # expect 403/404

  Apache honours the release's own .htaccess rules, so this should already hold.

  #### 2. Deploy the extension (not done by this manifest)

  The extension is deployed as a built archive, because dfc_civicrm has no git
  remote and its `vendor/` is gitignored. From your workstation:

      make ext-build      # runs the extension's own tools/build-release.sh
      make ext-install    # uploads the archive and runs cv ext:enable
      make ext-verify     # runs the extension's own tools/verify-install.sh

  #### 3. Add cron (one-time, not scriptable from JPS)

  Dashboard > CUSTOMER > Cron Jobs, using `ops/crontab`.

  #### 4. App server

  Apache is used deliberately: the CiviCRM release protects `private/` with
  `.htaccess`, which only Apache honours. Nothing further to configure.
